1
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,316
Hello, and welcome back to The Quiet Archive.

2
00:00:10,316 --> 00:00:21,075
Tonight we are going to spend a long, calm night with the people who kept the lights burning along the coast, about a hundred and sixty years ago.

3
00:00:21,075 --> 00:00:22,917
Nothing much will happen.

4
00:00:22,917 --> 00:00:30,949
A lamp will be lit, a lens will turn, a kettle will boil, and the sea will go on breathing against the rocks.

5
00:00:30,949 --> 00:00:34,413
You are welcome to drift off whenever you like.

6
00:00:36,013 --> 00:00:40,310
Before we begin, let your shoulders drop a little lower.

7
00:00:40,310 --> 00:00:43,149
Let your hands rest, heavy and still.

8
00:00:43,149 --> 00:00:49,979
Breathe in slowly through your nose, as if you were smelling cold, clean air off the sea.

9
00:00:49,979 --> 00:00:56,578
And let it go again, all the way out, the way a wave slides back down a shingle beach.

10
00:00:58,178 --> 00:01:07,434
We are on the far north coast of Scotland, in the eighteen sixties, on a headland of dark rock high above the water.

11
00:01:07,434 --> 00:01:13,817
A white tower stands at the very edge, with a cluster of low cottages beside it.

12
00:01:13,817 --> 00:01:22,434
The sun has just gone down behind the hills, and a long band of pale green light still lingers over the sea.

13
00:01:24,034 --> 00:01:28,198
For the rest of tonight, we will keep the watch together.

14
00:01:28,198 --> 00:01:37,695
We will climb the tower, light the lamp, sit beside the great glass lens, and look out across the dark water at the passing ships.

15
00:01:37,695 --> 00:01:43,320
And if you happen to fall asleep before morning, that is perfectly all right.

16
00:01:43,320 --> 00:01:46,242
The light will keep shining without you.

17
00:01:55,842 --> 00:02:06,016
The keepers' cottages are built of stone, thick and low against the wind, with slate roofs held down by heavy flags along the eaves.

18
00:02:06,016 --> 00:02:14,494
Their walls are whitewashed every summer, so that sailors can pick out the station by day as well as by night.

19
00:02:14,494 --> 00:02:22,125
A short path of crushed shells and gravel runs between the cottage doors and the base of the tower.

20
00:02:23,725 --> 00:02:31,450
Beyond the wall, the headland slopes away in short, springy turf, cropped close by rabbits and sheep.

21
00:02:31,450 --> 00:02:38,410
Clumps of sea pinks grow along the cliff edge, their pale flowers closed now for the night.

22
00:02:38,410 --> 00:02:48,813
Below, on a broad ledge of rock, a colony of seabirds has settled down to sleep, shuffling and murmuring softly, and then falling quiet.

23
00:02:50,413 --> 00:02:53,905
Around the buildings runs a high stone wall.

24
00:02:53,905 --> 00:02:59,223
Inside it, sheltered from the salt breeze, there is a small garden.

25
00:02:59,223 --> 00:03:08,827
Rows of potatoes and kale grow in the dark soil, and a few hardy cabbages, and a bed of rhubarb under an upturned bucket.

26
00:03:08,827 --> 00:03:17,400
In the corner stands a tidy pile of peat, cut from the moor and stacked to dry, ready for the kitchen fires.

27
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:20,778
Two families live here.

28
00:03:20,778 --> 00:03:29,439
The principal keeper has the larger cottage, nearest the tower, and his assistant has the smaller one beside it.

29
00:03:29,439 --> 00:03:40,187
In the eighteen sixties, a keeper here works for the Northern Lighthouse Board, which looks after every light around the coast of Scotland.

30
00:03:40,187 --> 00:03:47,842
He wears a dark uniform jacket with brass buttons, and he is expected to keep his station spotless.

31
00:03:49,442 --> 00:03:54,112
In the principal keeper's kitchen, the evening meal is over.

32
00:03:54,112 --> 00:04:02,752
His wife is wiping the table with a damp cloth, and the smell of oatcakes and peat smoke hangs in the warm air.

33
00:04:02,752 --> 00:04:08,045
A kettle sits at the back of the range, murmuring quietly to itself.

34
00:04:08,045 --> 00:04:13,805
On the windowsill, a row of geraniums leans towards the last of the light.

35
00:04:15,405 --> 00:04:18,939
Their children have been sent to wash their faces.

36
00:04:18,939 --> 00:04:30,106
There are three of them, and they go to a little school a few miles down the road, walking there in the mornings with their satchels and their oatcake pieces.

37
00:04:30,106 --> 00:04:36,467
In the long summer evenings they collect gulls' feathers and smooth stones from the shore.

38
00:04:36,467 --> 00:04:40,920
Tonight they are tired, and their beds are already turned down.

39
00:04:42,520 --> 00:04:52,699
Out in the byre, a single cow is chewing slowly, and a few hens have settled on their perch in a row, feathers puffed against the cool.

40
00:04:52,699 --> 00:05:01,899
The keepers are allowed a patch of grazing on the headland, and most stations keep a cow for milk and a few hens for eggs.

41
00:05:01,899 --> 00:05:08,610
The nearest shop is a long walk away, so a lighthouse family learns to look after itself.

42
00:05:10,210 --> 00:05:16,956
There is a sundial on a stone pillar in the garden, and a tall flagstaff by the wall.

43
00:05:16,956 --> 00:05:23,067
Inside the principal keeper's cottage, a good clock ticks on the mantelpiece.

44
00:05:23,067 --> 00:05:25,845
The keepers are careful about time.

45
00:05:25,845 --> 00:05:35,448
The lamp must be lit at sunset, exactly, and put out at sunrise, exactly, and every one of those minutes is written down.

46
00:05:37,048 --> 00:05:47,362
On washing day, the keepers' wives hang the sheets out on a line between two posts, and the wind off the sea snaps them dry in an hour.

47
00:05:47,362 --> 00:05:51,870
Tonight the line is empty, swinging a little in the breeze.

48
00:05:51,870 --> 00:06:00,962
A pair of small boots stands upside down on the doorstep, drying, and a fishing rod leans against the wall beside them.

49
00:06:02,562 --> 00:06:10,510
Now the green glow over the sea is fading into blue, and the first stars are coming out above the tower.

50
00:06:10,510 --> 00:06:15,095
The principal keeper looks up at the sky, then at the clock.

51
00:06:15,095 --> 00:06:23,501
He takes his jacket from the hook behind the door, buttons it neatly, and steps out into the cool evening air.

52
00:06:23,501 --> 00:06:24,877
It is almost time.

53
00:06:34,477 --> 00:06:39,987
The door at the foot of the tower is heavy and painted a deep green.

54
00:06:39,987 --> 00:06:45,903
Inside, it is cool and quiet, and smells of stone and paint and lamp oil.

55
00:06:45,903 --> 00:06:57,815
A spiral stair of stone steps winds up and up around the inside of the wall, each step worn a little smooth in the middle by years of patient feet.

56
00:06:59,416 --> 00:07:01,750
Follow the keeper as he climbs.

57
00:07:01,750 --> 00:07:08,227
His boots ring softly on the steps, and his hand slides along the polished brass rail.

58
00:07:08,227 --> 00:07:17,640
Here and there a small window lets in a square of fading blue, and you glimpse the sea far below, grey and wrinkled and calm.

59
00:07:17,640 --> 00:07:24,418
Round and round you go, a little higher with every turn, a little further from the ground.

60
00:07:26,018 --> 00:07:28,658
Partway up, you pass the oil room.

61
00:07:28,658 --> 00:07:41,078
Rows of large metal cans stand along the wall, filled with colza oil, pressed from the seeds of a yellow-flowered plant, a cousin of the cabbage and the turnip.

62
00:07:41,078 --> 00:07:45,581
It burns with a clear, steady flame and very little smoke.

63
00:07:45,581 --> 00:07:52,024
In a few years, many lights will change to paraffin, which is brighter and cheaper.

64
00:07:52,024 --> 00:07:53,965
But tonight, it is colza.

65
00:07:55,565 --> 00:08:02,640
Near the top of the stair is the service room, a small round room just beneath the lantern.

66
00:08:02,640 --> 00:08:10,182
Here the keeper keeps his tools, his spare wicks, his soft cloths, his brushes, and a tall clock.

67
00:08:10,182 --> 00:08:20,290
A narrow iron ladder leads up through a hatch in the ceiling into the lantern itself, the glass room at the very top of the tower.

68
00:08:21,890 --> 00:08:24,368
Everything here is kept gleaming.

69
00:08:24,368 --> 00:08:31,052
The brass handrail, the door handles, the oil cans, the little brass plates on the clock.

70
00:08:31,052 --> 00:08:37,586
Polishing is part of every keeper's day, and every room is cleaned to a strict routine.

71
00:08:37,586 --> 00:08:49,752
Now and then, without warning, a superintendent arrives on an inspection, and runs a white glove along the top of a doorframe, and expects to find no dust at all.

72
00:08:51,352 --> 00:08:55,342
He climbs the ladder and steps up into the lantern.

73
00:08:55,342 --> 00:09:05,201
All around him are tall windows of thick glass, set in a frame of bronze, and through them the whole sky is turning deep blue.

74
00:09:05,201 --> 00:09:15,608
At the centre of the room stands the lens, taller than a man, covered all day long by a linen cloth to keep off the dust and the sun.

75
00:09:17,208 --> 00:09:22,783
During the day, the keepers draw curtains around the inside of the lantern.

76
00:09:22,783 --> 00:09:36,165
A great lens can gather the sunlight just as easily as it gathers lamplight, and if the curtains were left open, the summer sun could scorch the brass and crack the delicate wicks.

77
00:09:36,165 --> 00:09:44,194
So every morning the curtains are closed, and every evening, at lighting-up time, they are drawn back again.

78
00:09:45,794 --> 00:09:51,545
The keeper lifts the cloth from the lens and folds it neatly over his arm.

79
00:09:51,545 --> 00:09:55,741
He checks the clock, and the sky, and the clock again.

80
00:09:55,741 --> 00:10:04,911
Then he opens a small glass door in the side of the lens, reaches in with a lighted taper, and touches it to the wick.

81
00:10:04,911 --> 00:10:09,496
A small blue flame runs around the ring, and turns to gold.

82
00:10:11,096 --> 00:10:13,416
He waits, watching the flame.

83
00:10:13,416 --> 00:10:24,536
Slowly he turns the little brass wheel that raises the wick, a fraction at a time, until the flame stands tall and even and does not smoke.

84
00:10:24,536 --> 00:10:26,936
Then he closes the glass door.

85
00:10:26,936 --> 00:10:37,016
Outside, far across the water, a fisherman on his way home looks up and sees the light come on, and knows exactly where he is.

86
00:10:38,616 --> 00:10:44,154
The keeper writes the time in a small notebook, ready to copy into the log.

87
00:10:44,154 --> 00:10:52,055
Then he steps back and looks at the lens, glowing softly now from within, as if it were full of warm honey.

88
00:10:52,055 --> 00:11:02,466
Through the windows, the sky is a deep, clear blue, and the sea has turned to ink, and the first beam of the night is reaching out across it.

89
00:11:12,066 --> 00:11:14,660
The lamp itself is not very large.

90
00:11:14,660 --> 00:11:22,520
If you took it out of the lens and set it on a kitchen table, it would light a room, and not much more.

91
00:11:22,520 --> 00:11:32,134
It has several round wicks, one inside another, like the rings of a tree, so that a broad, bright flame rises from the centre.

92
00:11:32,134 --> 00:11:36,941
But on its own, it could never be seen twenty miles out at sea.

93
00:11:38,541 --> 00:11:41,551
What makes the difference is the glass.

94
00:11:41,551 --> 00:11:51,970
Around the lamp stands a tall cage of brass, and fixed in the cage are hundreds of curved pieces of glass, ground and polished by hand.

95
00:11:51,970 --> 00:11:59,224
Some sit in rings above the flame, and some below, and some in a broad belt around the middle.

96
00:11:59,224 --> 00:12:04,781
Together they look like a great glass beehive, or a lantern made of ice.

97
00:12:06,381 --> 00:12:13,250
The idea came from a quiet French engineer named Augustin Fresnel, in the eighteen twenties.

98
00:12:13,250 --> 00:12:26,765
Instead of one enormous, heavy lens, he built his out of many thin rings and prisms, each one shaped to catch a little of the lamplight and bend it, gently, into a single direction.

99
00:12:26,765 --> 00:12:35,352
Light that would have wandered off into the sky or down into the ground is gathered up and sent out across the sea.

100
00:12:36,951 --> 00:12:40,039
Stand beside it now, and look closely.

101
00:12:40,039 --> 00:12:48,002
The glass is so clear you can hardly see it, except where the edges of the prisms catch the flame.

102
00:12:48,002 --> 00:12:53,852
Every ring holds a tiny golden image of the lamp, and every image glows.

103
00:12:53,852 --> 00:13:03,277
Tiny rainbows tremble along the edges of the glass, violet and green and gold, and slide away as you move your head.

104
00:13:04,877 --> 00:13:07,365
The lens is very slowly turning.

105
00:13:07,365 --> 00:13:17,471
It rests on a bed of rollers, and beneath it, in the service room, a clockwork machine of brass wheels is pushing it gently round.

106
00:13:17,471 --> 00:13:23,613
The clockwork is driven by a heavy weight, hanging on a cable inside the tower.

107
00:13:23,613 --> 00:13:30,221
As the weight sinks, the wheels turn, and the great lens revolves, steady and silent.

108
00:13:31,821 --> 00:13:39,100
Every few hours, the weight reaches the bottom of its fall, and the keeper must wind it up again.

109
00:13:39,100 --> 00:13:49,080
He fits a crank handle to the machine and turns it, round and round, with a slow, patient rhythm, and a soft clicking of the ratchet.

110
00:13:49,080 --> 00:13:55,309
Then the weight is raised, and the lens can turn for another few hours without him.

111
00:13:56,909 --> 00:14:03,590
In the middle of the lens there are broad round panels of glass, like great glass eyes.

112
00:14:03,590 --> 00:14:11,117
As each one swings past, the light gathers into a single bright beam that sweeps across the water.

113
00:14:11,117 --> 00:14:22,637
Out at sea, a sailor sees a sudden, steady flash, and then darkness, and then another flash, all in a pattern that belongs to this light and no other.

114
00:14:24,237 --> 00:14:36,630
Inside the lantern, the turning lens throws slow patterns of light across the walls and the ceiling, bands of gold that drift past and fade and come round again.

115
00:14:36,630 --> 00:14:48,791
If you stand still and watch them for a while, they are almost hypnotic, like the light from a fire, or the reflections of water on the underside of a bridge.

116
00:14:50,391 --> 00:14:55,143
Every lighthouse has its own character, as the keepers call it.

117
00:14:55,143 --> 00:14:59,367
One might show a single white flash every half a minute.

118
00:14:59,367 --> 00:15:02,082
Another, two flashes close together.

119
00:15:02,082 --> 00:15:05,476
Another, a light that slowly grows and fades.

120
00:15:05,476 --> 00:15:07,814
Another, red and white in turn.

121
00:15:07,814 --> 00:15:19,127
A ship's captain with his book of lights can count the seconds between the flashes, and know, even on the darkest night, which headland he is passing.

122
00:15:28,727 --> 00:15:32,904
Now the light is burning, and the long night watch begins.

123
00:15:32,904 --> 00:15:46,803
The keeper settles at a small desk in the service room, beneath the lantern, where he can hear the soft tick of the clockwork above his head and see the glow of the lamp through the open hatch.

124
00:15:46,803 --> 00:15:54,221
The watches are shared between the keepers, so that one man is always awake while the light is shining.

125
00:15:55,821 --> 00:16:03,334
On the desk lies the logbook, a heavy book bound in dark cloth, with ruled columns on every page.

126
00:16:03,334 --> 00:16:10,072
In neat, careful handwriting, the keeper writes the date, and the time he lit the lamp.

127
00:16:10,072 --> 00:16:15,184
Then the direction of the wind, the state of the sea, the weather.

128
00:16:15,184 --> 00:16:17,198
Fine and clear, he writes.

129
00:16:17,198 --> 00:16:19,289
Light breeze from the west.

130
00:16:19,289 --> 00:16:20,141
Sea smooth.

131
00:16:21,741 --> 00:16:26,835
Every so often, he climbs back up into the lantern to check the flame.

132
00:16:26,835 --> 00:16:35,130
Over the hours, the wicks slowly char at the top, and the flame begins to droop, or to smoke a little at one side.

133
00:16:35,130 --> 00:16:45,100
So he trims them, carefully, with a small pair of scissors, cutting away the blackened edge until the flame stands bright and even again.

134
00:16:45,100 --> 00:16:50,775
In America, keepers were sometimes nicknamed wickies, for exactly this reason.

135
00:16:52,375 --> 00:16:55,894
Between these small tasks, there is time to sit.

136
00:16:55,894 --> 00:17:03,225
The keeper fills his pipe from a tin on the desk, and lights it, and leans back in his wooden chair.

137
00:17:03,225 --> 00:17:15,394
Through the little window beside him, he can see the beam of the lighthouse sweeping out across the water, catching the tops of the waves for a moment, and moving on.

138
00:17:16,994 --> 00:17:21,175
On the stove in the corner, a small kettle is warming.

139
00:17:21,175 --> 00:17:28,762
When it starts to sing, he pours the water into a brown teapot, and waits, and then fills his mug.

140
00:17:28,762 --> 00:17:34,724
He drinks it slowly, with both hands around the mug, looking out at the dark.

141
00:17:34,724 --> 00:17:40,375
The tea tastes of smoke and milk and a little sugar, and it is very good.

142
00:17:41,976 --> 00:17:46,000
Sometimes, on these quiet watches, a keeper reads.

143
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:56,383
A book of sermons, perhaps, or a volume of travels, or a newspaper that came with the last delivery and is already two weeks old.

144
00:17:56,383 --> 00:18:05,720
He reads by the small oil lamp on the desk, a page at a time, looking up at the end of each page to check the light.

145
00:18:07,320 --> 00:18:10,807
The tower makes its own small sounds at night.

146
00:18:10,807 --> 00:18:12,475
The tick of the clock.

147
00:18:12,475 --> 00:18:16,417
The hum of the wind across the gallery rail outside.

148
00:18:16,417 --> 00:18:18,995
The soft rush of the lamp's flame.

149
00:18:18,995 --> 00:18:23,922
Now and then a creak from somewhere in the stonework as it cools.

150
00:18:23,922 --> 00:18:29,912
The keeper knows every one of these sounds, and they tell him that all is well.

151
00:18:31,512 --> 00:18:42,430
Moths sometimes find their way up to the lantern on summer nights, drawn by the glow, and flutter softly against the outside of the glass.

152
00:18:42,430 --> 00:18:45,041
Tonight it is too cool for moths.

153
00:18:45,041 --> 00:18:56,514
Instead, a small brown bird has tucked itself into a corner of the gallery outside, out of the wind, with its head beneath its wing, fast asleep.

154
00:18:58,114 --> 00:19:02,611
Below, in the cottages, the lights have gone out one by one.

155
00:19:02,611 --> 00:19:10,930
The assistant keeper is asleep, because he will take the middle watch, from midnight until four in the morning.

156
00:19:10,930 --> 00:19:23,672
His wife is asleep beside him, and the children are asleep in the little room under the roof, and the cow in the byre has folded her legs beneath her and closed her eyes.

157
00:19:25,272 --> 00:19:28,660
The keeper writes another line in the log.

158
00:19:28,660 --> 00:19:29,628
Ten o'clock.

159
00:19:29,628 --> 00:19:31,161
Light burning well.

160
00:19:31,161 --> 00:19:32,855
Wind westerly, light.

161
00:19:32,855 --> 00:19:43,746
Then he puts down the pen, wipes it on a scrap of cloth, and sits back to watch the slow beam turning, turning, out over the quiet sea.

162
00:19:53,346 --> 00:19:58,990
Far out on the horizon, beyond the headland, another light is flashing.

163
00:19:58,990 --> 00:20:07,733
It belongs to a different kind of lighthouse altogether, a rock station, built on a reef many miles from land.

164
00:20:07,733 --> 00:20:11,628
There is no garden there, no cottage, and no cow.

165
00:20:11,628 --> 00:20:15,682
There is only the tower, and the sea all around it.

166
00:20:17,282 --> 00:20:21,739
Some of these towers are among the great works of their age.

167
00:20:21,739 --> 00:20:30,506
The Bell Rock, off the east coast of Scotland, rises from a reef that disappears beneath the water at every high tide.

168
00:20:30,506 --> 00:20:44,546
It was built by Robert Stevenson and his men between eighteen hundred and seven and eighteen hundred and ten, stone by stone, in the few hours each day when the rock stood clear of the sea.

169
00:20:46,146 --> 00:20:56,508
Off the west coast, beyond the island of Tiree, stands Skerryvore, designed by his son Alan and first lit in eighteen forty four.

170
00:20:56,508 --> 00:21:09,200
It is a tall, slender tower of granite, curving gently outward at the base like the trunk of a great tree, so that the waves slide up around it and fall away.

171
00:21:09,200 --> 00:21:12,493
It is the tallest lighthouse in Scotland.

172
00:21:14,093 --> 00:21:19,411
Inside a rock tower, everything is stacked, one room on top of the next.

173
00:21:19,411 --> 00:21:22,071
At the bottom, water tanks and coal.

174
00:21:22,071 --> 00:21:23,991
Above that, the oil store.

175
00:21:23,991 --> 00:21:26,651
Then a store room for food and rope.

176
00:21:26,651 --> 00:21:30,122
Then a kitchen, with a small stove and a table.

177
00:21:30,122 --> 00:21:31,230
Then a bedroom.

178
00:21:31,230 --> 00:21:35,367
Then the service room, and at the very top, the lantern.

179
00:21:35,367 --> 00:21:40,760
Each room is round, and joined to the next by a ladder or a narrow stair.

180
00:21:42,360 --> 00:21:57,449
The walls at the base of a rock tower are enormously thick, made of great blocks of stone cut to fit together like the pieces of a puzzle, each one locked to its neighbours above and below and on either side.

181
00:21:57,449 --> 00:22:02,454
Higher up, the walls grow thinner, and the rooms grow a little wider.

182
00:22:02,454 --> 00:22:09,709
The whole tower is shaped to stand quietly, year after year, while the sea comes and goes around it.

183
00:22:11,309 --> 00:22:14,972
Because the walls are round, the beds are curved.

184
00:22:14,972 --> 00:22:24,392
They are built into the wall like long wooden boxes, bending gently to follow the stone, each with a curtain across the front.

185
00:22:24,392 --> 00:22:27,233
Keepers have called them banana bunks.

186
00:22:27,233 --> 00:22:36,205
A man lying in one at night can hear the sea moving around the foot of the tower, a long way below, like slow breathing.

187
00:22:37,805 --> 00:22:44,757
Three keepers live on a rock station at a time, with a fourth ashore having his turn of rest.

188
00:22:44,757 --> 00:22:55,747
Every few weeks, if the weather allows, a small steamer comes out from the shore station, bringing fresh food, water, letters, and a relief keeper.

189
00:22:55,747 --> 00:23:04,045
One man climbs down the ladder into the boat to go home to his family, and another climbs up to take his place.

190
00:23:05,645 --> 00:23:14,646
Ashore, the families of the rock keepers live together at a shore station, in a row of cottages near the harbour.

191
00:23:14,646 --> 00:23:30,498
At some stations, a signal tower stands on the shore, so that messages can be passed back and forth, by flags or by signals raised on a mast, between the families on land and the men out on the rock.

192
00:23:32,098 --> 00:23:34,979
Each man takes his turn at the cooking.

193
00:23:34,979 --> 00:23:44,361
They bake their own bread in the little oven, and the smell of it rises up through the rooms of the tower, warm and comforting.

194
00:23:44,361 --> 00:23:56,994
In the evenings there are games of draughts and cribbage at the kitchen table, and books from a wooden library box, which is changed for a new box of books at each relief.

195
00:23:58,594 --> 00:24:01,963
Some keepers make things to pass the hours.

196
00:24:01,963 --> 00:24:05,254
Rag rugs, hooked from scraps of old cloth.

197
00:24:05,254 --> 00:24:09,015
Little models of ships, rigged with fine thread.

198
00:24:09,015 --> 00:24:11,523
Carved boxes and picture frames.

199
00:24:11,523 --> 00:24:19,828
On calm days, some of them fish from the gallery, letting a long line down into the green water far below.

200
00:24:19,828 --> 00:24:23,981
And all the while, the light turns above their heads.

201
00:24:33,581 --> 00:24:42,301
Out on the water, a small sailing ship is passing, a little schooner with two masts, heading south along the coast.

202
00:24:42,301 --> 00:24:45,411
Her sails are pale grey in the starlight.

203
00:24:45,411 --> 00:24:59,970
At her stern, a single lantern glows, and on either side of her bow there is a coloured light, red on the left side and green on the right, so that other ships can tell which way she is going.

204
00:25:01,570 --> 00:25:07,714
On her deck, a sailor stands at the wheel, his collar turned up against the cool air.

205
00:25:07,714 --> 00:25:17,689
He has been watching the lighthouse for the last hour, counting the flashes, checking them against the book of lights in the little cabin.

206
00:25:17,689 --> 00:25:20,074
He knows exactly where he is now.

207
00:25:20,074 --> 00:25:24,845
The rocks are well to the west of him, and the way ahead is clear.

208
00:25:26,445 --> 00:25:31,276
For him, the beam sweeping across the water is a kind of company.

209
00:25:31,276 --> 00:25:44,431
Out here, with only the creak of the rigging and the wash of the bow, it is good to know that someone on that dark headland is awake, and keeping the lamp trimmed, and watching.

210
00:25:44,431 --> 00:25:49,634
He raises a hand towards the light, though no one can possibly see it.

211
00:25:51,234 --> 00:25:59,634
In the lantern, the keeper sees the schooner too, a tiny cluster of lights moving slowly across the black water.

212
00:25:59,634 --> 00:26:01,885
He watches it for a long time.

213
00:26:01,885 --> 00:26:07,135
He does not know her name, or where she is going, or what she carries.

214
00:26:07,135 --> 00:26:11,185
Perhaps coal, or timber, or barrels of salted herring.

215
00:26:11,185 --> 00:26:12,610
It does not matter.

216
00:26:12,610 --> 00:26:17,410
She is passing safely, and that is the whole point of the light.

217
00:26:19,010 --> 00:26:23,717
On a clear night like this, the keepers see many ships go by.

218
00:26:23,717 --> 00:26:29,582
Fishing boats heading out for the night, with their nets piled in the stern.

219
00:26:29,582 --> 00:26:35,138
Coastal steamers with a plume of smoke and a row of small lit portholes.

220
00:26:35,138 --> 00:26:45,016
Now and then, a great sailing ship from far away, her sails stacked high like clouds, gliding past on her way to a distant port.

221
00:26:46,616 --> 00:26:50,195
Some of those ships have been at sea for months.

222
00:26:50,195 --> 00:26:56,086
Their crews have watched the stars and the compass and the colour of the water.

223
00:26:56,086 --> 00:27:04,735
And then, one evening, low on the horizon, a tiny light appears, flashing in a pattern they recognise from the book.

224
00:27:04,735 --> 00:27:10,552
It is the first sign of home, and the men gather at the rail to watch it grow.

225
00:27:12,152 --> 00:27:18,154
Over the years, the keepers come to know the coast as well as their own kitchens.

226
00:27:18,154 --> 00:27:20,822
Every rock and current and headland.

227
00:27:20,822 --> 00:27:31,197
Every light along the shore, flashing in its own rhythm, one here, another there, far off along the coast, all keeping the same quiet watch.

228
00:27:31,197 --> 00:27:40,461
On some nights, from the top of a tall tower, a keeper can see three or four other lighthouses at once, like distant friends.

229
00:27:42,061 --> 00:27:44,682
The keepers know the sea birds too.

230
00:27:44,682 --> 00:27:50,000
The gannets, folding their wings and diving like arrows into the water.

231
00:27:50,000 --> 00:27:55,618
The puffins on the cliffs in early summer, with their bright striped beaks.

232
00:27:55,618 --> 00:27:58,239
The eider ducks rocking in the bay.

233
00:27:58,239 --> 00:28:09,474
And on autumn nights, flocks of small birds on their long journey south, which sometimes rest for a little while on the gallery rail before flying on.

234
00:28:11,074 --> 00:28:17,613
The schooner is further off now, her stern lantern a small gold point on the dark water.

235
00:28:17,613 --> 00:28:22,591
The swell lifts her gently, and lets her down, and lifts her again.

236
00:28:22,591 --> 00:28:32,770
Soon she will round the next headland, and the next light will take up the watch, and guide her on through the night towards the morning.

237
00:28:34,370 --> 00:28:36,728
The keeper writes in the log.

238
00:28:36,728 --> 00:28:37,948
Eleven o'clock.

239
00:28:37,948 --> 00:28:40,469
One schooner passing southward.

240
00:28:40,469 --> 00:28:53,805
Then he looks up again, through the tall glass windows, at the beam sweeping slowly out across the empty sea, and in again, and out again, as steady as a heartbeat.

241
00:29:03,405 --> 00:29:09,556
Just before midnight, there is a soft knock on the door at the foot of the tower.

242
00:29:09,556 --> 00:29:13,732
The assistant keeper has come to take the middle watch.

243
00:29:13,732 --> 00:29:25,122
He climbs the long spiral stair, yawning, his lamp in his hand, and comes up into the service room with the cool night air still clinging to his coat.

244
00:29:26,722 --> 00:29:30,129
The two men speak quietly for a few minutes.

245
00:29:30,129 --> 00:29:32,142
The light is burning well.

246
00:29:32,142 --> 00:29:35,161
The clockwork has been wound at eleven.

247
00:29:35,161 --> 00:29:38,491
The wind is light and steady from the west.

248
00:29:38,491 --> 00:29:40,891
A schooner went by an hour ago.

249
00:29:40,891 --> 00:29:51,576
Then the first keeper signs the log, puts on his cap, and goes down the stair, round and round, towards his warm bed in the cottage below.

250
00:29:53,176 --> 00:29:56,862
Now the assistant keeper is alone in the tower.

251
00:29:56,862 --> 00:30:05,412
He climbs up into the lantern and stands beside the great lens for a while, warming his hands near the glass.

252
00:30:05,412 --> 00:30:10,510
Close to the lamp, the air is warm and smells faintly of hot oil.

253
00:30:10,510 --> 00:30:19,138
A little further away, near the windows, it is cool, and the glass is beaded with a fine mist of condensation.

254
00:30:20,738 --> 00:30:33,597
He takes a soft chamois leather from his pocket and walks slowly round the inside of the lantern, wiping each pane of glass in turn, so that nothing will dim the light.

255
00:30:33,597 --> 00:30:44,696
Through each pane, as it clears, the night appears again, the dark water, the pale line of foam along the rocks, the stars crowding over the sea.

256
00:30:46,296 --> 00:30:59,997
Every morning, after the light is put out, the keepers will clean the lens itself, with soft linen and a little spirit, prism by prism, ring by ring, without a single scratch.

257
00:30:59,997 --> 00:31:02,189
It can take an hour or more.

258
00:31:02,189 --> 00:31:10,018
A keeper polishes the same glass every day of his working life, and is quietly proud that it shines.

259
00:31:11,618 --> 00:31:21,746
Then he steps out through a small door onto the gallery, the narrow iron balcony that runs right around the outside of the lantern.

260
00:31:21,746 --> 00:31:26,152
The air is cold and fresh and smells of salt and seaweed.

261
00:31:26,152 --> 00:31:36,280
Far below, the waves are folding quietly against the foot of the cliff, a long, slow hush, and then a pause, and then another hush.

262
00:31:37,880 --> 00:31:40,438
Above him, the sky is enormous.

263
00:31:40,438 --> 00:31:52,242
With no towns nearby, the stars are thick and bright, and the pale river of the Milky Way runs right across the sky from one side to the other.

264
00:31:52,242 --> 00:32:03,138
Behind him, the beam swings out over his head, a broad pale arm of light reaching far across the water, and slowly turns away again.

265
00:32:04,738 --> 00:32:14,319
Low in the north, there is a faint, pale glow above the horizon, soft and shifting, perhaps the first stirring of the northern lights.

266
00:32:14,319 --> 00:32:21,541
It brightens a little, and fades, and brightens again, and the keeper watches it with quiet pleasure.

267
00:32:21,541 --> 00:32:32,194
He has seen the lights many times from this gallery, curtains of green and rose moving silently over the sea, and they still make him stop and stare.

268
00:32:33,794 --> 00:32:43,322
He stays out there for a little while, his hands resting on the cold rail, breathing the sea air, watching the beam go round.

269
00:32:43,322 --> 00:32:49,724
This is the part of the job many keepers love most, though few of them would say so.

270
00:32:49,724 --> 00:32:50,487
The quiet.

271
00:32:50,487 --> 00:32:51,249
The stars.

272
00:32:51,249 --> 00:32:56,813
The feeling of being awake and useful while the rest of the world sleeps.

273
00:32:58,413 --> 00:33:05,349
Then he goes back inside, closes the little door behind him, and sits down at the desk.

274
00:33:05,349 --> 00:33:12,365
He winds the clockwork, slowly, round and round, with that soft clicking of the ratchet.

275
00:33:12,365 --> 00:33:13,800
He trims the wick.

276
00:33:13,800 --> 00:33:15,475
He writes in the log.

277
00:33:15,475 --> 00:33:16,431
One o'clock.

278
00:33:16,431 --> 00:33:17,149
All well.

279
00:33:17,149 --> 00:33:20,258
And outside, the light goes on turning.

280
00:33:29,858 --> 00:33:33,482
Down in the cottages, everyone is sleeping now.

281
00:33:33,482 --> 00:33:39,497
The principal keeper lies on his back with his hands folded, breathing slowly.

282
00:33:39,497 --> 00:33:42,967
His wife has pulled the quilt up to her chin.

283
00:33:42,967 --> 00:33:53,069
The children are curled up together under the roof, and the peat fire in the kitchen has sunk to a soft red glow beneath its ashes.

284
00:33:54,669 --> 00:34:08,470
Far out at sea, on the rock station, the keepers are sleeping too, in their curved wooden bunks, with the curtains drawn, and the slow sound of the sea around the foot of the tower.

285
00:34:08,470 --> 00:34:19,373
Only one man in each tower is awake, sitting quietly beneath the lantern, with a mug of tea going cold beside him and the log open on the desk.

286
00:34:20,973 --> 00:34:28,685
Let yourself rest now, like the families in the cottages, warm and safe while someone else keeps watch.

287
00:34:28,685 --> 00:34:31,455
You do not need to count the flashes.

288
00:34:31,455 --> 00:34:36,921
You do not need to trim the wick, or wind the clock, or write in the log.

289
00:34:36,921 --> 00:34:39,092
All of that is taken care of.

290
00:34:39,092 --> 00:34:44,034
All you need to do is breathe, slowly, and let the light go round.

291
00:34:45,634 --> 00:34:49,290
In, as the beam swings out across the water.

292
00:34:49,290 --> 00:34:51,699
Out, as it turns slowly away.

293
00:34:51,699 --> 00:34:52,696
In, and out.

294
00:34:52,696 --> 00:35:09,229
The sea breathes against the rocks below, and the stars turn slowly overhead, and the great lens turns with them, patient and steady, all through the long night, until the sky grows pale in the east.

295
00:35:10,829 --> 00:35:15,975
Thank you for keeping the watch with The Quiet Archive tonight.

296
00:35:15,975 --> 00:35:22,020
The lamp is lit, the lens is turning, and the ships are passing safely by.

297
00:35:22,020 --> 00:35:22,919
Sleep well.

298
00:35:22,919 --> 00:35:23,736
Goodnight.
